Given curve is
$y=(1+x)^{y}+\sin ^{-1}\left(\sin ^{2} x\right)$
On differentiating w.r.t $x$, we get
$\begin{array}{l}
\frac{\mathrm{dy}}{\mathrm{dx}}=(1+\mathrm{x})^{\mathrm{y}}\left[\frac{\mathrm{y}}{1+\mathrm{x}}+\log (1+\mathrm{x}) \frac{\mathrm{dy}}{\mathrm{dx}}\right] \\
\Rightarrow\left(\frac{\mathrm{dy}}{\mathrm{dx}}\right)_{\mathrm{at}(0,1)}=\frac{2 \sin \mathrm{x} \cos \mathrm{x}}{\sqrt{1-\sin ^{4} \mathrm{x}}}
\end{array}
Slope of normal at $(x=0)=-1\therefore \quad$ Equation of normal at $x=0$ and $y=1$ is $\begin{aligned} y-1 &=-1(x-0) \\ \Rightarrow y-1 &=-x \Rightarrow x+y=1 \end{aligned}

Given, $\sin y=x \sin (a+y)$
\Rightarrow \quad x=\frac{\sin y}{\sin (a+y)}

On differentiating w.r. to $y$, we get $\frac{d x}{d y}=\frac{d}{d y}\left[\frac{\sin y}{\sin (a+y)}\right]$
\begin{array}{l}
=\frac{\sin (a+y) \cos y-\sin y \cos (a+y)}{\sin ^{2}(a+y)} \\
=\frac{\sin a}{\sin ^{2}(a+y)} \Rightarrow \frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{\sin ^{2}(a+y)}{\sin a}
\end{array}

\begin{aligned}
y &=\tan ^{-1} \frac{4 x}{1+5 x^{2}}+\tan ^{-1} \frac{2+3 x}{3-2 x} \\
&=\tan ^{-1} \frac{5 x-x}{1+5 x \cdot x}+\tan ^{-1} \frac{\frac{2}{3}+x}{1-\frac{2}{3} \cdot x} \\
&=\tan ^{-1} 5 x-\tan ^{-1} x+\tan ^{-1} \frac{2}{3}+\tan ^{-1} x \\
\Rightarrow & \frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{5}{1+25 x^{2}}
\end{aligned}

Put \(x=\sin ^2 \theta\) and \(a=\sin ^2 \phi\). then
\(\begin{aligned}
& y=\sin ^{-1}(\sin \theta \cos \phi-\cos \theta \sin \phi) \\
& =\sin ^{-1} \sqrt{x}-\sin ^{-1} \sqrt{a} \\
& \therefore \quad \frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x}} \cdot \frac{1}{2 \sqrt{x}}
\end{aligned}\)

Let $P\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\right)$ be a point on the curve

y^{2}=4 a x...(i)

On differentiating $y^{2}=4 a x$ w.r.t. $^{\prime} x^{\prime}$, we get

\begin{array}{l}
2 y \frac{d y}{d x}=4 a \\
\Rightarrow\left(\frac{d y}{d x}\right)_{\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\right)}=\frac{2 a}{y_{1}}
\end{array}

Thus, the equation of normal at $\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\right)$ is
$y-y_{1}=-\frac{y_{1}}{2 a}\left(x-x_{1}\right)\Rightarrow y_{1} x+2 a y=y_{1}\left(x_{1}+2 a\right)$...(i)
But $l x+m y=1$ ...(ii)
is alsoa normal. Therefore, coefficients of eqs. (ii) and (iii), must be proportional. i.e., $\frac{y_{1}}{l}=\frac{2 a}{m}=\frac{y_{1}\left(x_{1}+2 a\right)}{1}\Rightarrow y_{1}=\frac{2 a l}{m}$ and $x_{1}=\frac{1}{l}-2 a$ must be proportional.

Putting these values of $x_{1}$ and $y_{1}$ in eq. (i), we get

\begin{array}{l}
\left(\frac{2 a l}{m}\right)^{2}=4 a\left(\frac{1}{l}-2 a\right) \\
\Rightarrow \frac{4 a^{2} l^{2}}{m^{2}}=\frac{4 a-8 a^{2} l}{l} \\
\Rightarrow a l^{3}=m^{2}-2 a m^{2} l \Rightarrow a l^{3}+2 a l m^{2}=m^{2}
\end{array}
